Q1. What will be the output of the following code snippet?
a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]
print(a[::2])
A1.The output will be [1, 3, 5, 7, 9].

Q2. What will be the output of the following code snippet?
l = [1, 2, 3]
init_tuple = ('Python',) * (l.__len__() - l[::-1][0])
print(init_tuple)
A2.The output is an empty tuple: ()
Reason: l.__len__() is 3. The code l[::-1] reverses the list to [3, 2, 1], and getting the [0] index of that reversed list gives us 3. 
So, the calculation is (3 - 3), which equals 0. If you multiply a tuple by 0, it empties it out.

